From openbsd-alpha  Sat Nov 16 00:03:04 2002
From: Miod Vallat <miod () online ! fr>
Date: Sat, 16 Nov 2002 00:03:04 +0000
To: openbsd-alpha
Subject: Re: SX164 and 3.2 - fails to give login prompt
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=openbsd-alpha&m=103748394421579

> I have just done a clean install of 3.2 on my SX164. Previously running 3.0
> after some minor initial "teething problems".
[...]
> Reboot to multi-user results in an apparent "hang" without getting to the
> login prompt (see below for details).
> The machine is "alive" however, and can be pinged and logged into via the
> network (again see below - I enabled telnetd for testing purposes).
[...]
> com0 at isa0 port 0x3f8/8 irq 4: ns16550a, 16 byte fifo
> com0: console

You are running on serial console.
There is a known problem on alpha, where no getty is listening on serial
ports by default.

You can fix this by editing /etc/ttys and enable the tty00 (and tty01 if
you want to make use of the second serial port too) entries.

Miod

From openbsd-alpha  Fri Nov 15 13:10:01 2002
From: Peter Rye <prye () shiraz ! apana ! org ! au>
Date: Fri, 15 Nov 2002 13:10:01 +0000
To: openbsd-alpha
Subject: Re: SX164 and 3.2 - "hang" on boot - solved
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=openbsd-alpha&m=103736565601281

Sorry to follow up on my own post.

As sometimes happens (to me anyway) on further thinking about the problem
as I was writing my last post, it has become clear what the cause for this
"problem" was.

I had neglected to edit the /etc/ttys file to enable the getty on the
serial line I was using. This should have been obvious to me.

At least I was right about it being a simple problem!

Regards,
Peter Rye

From openbsd-alpha  Wed Nov 13 13:41:57 2002
From: Paul de Weerd <paul () mail ! me ! maar ! nu>
Date: Wed, 13 Nov 2002 13:41:57 +0000
To: openbsd-alpha
Subject: X on Voodoo Banshee
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=openbsd-alpha&m=103719503100610

All,

After some issues with my external SCSI-cabinet I am reporting to the
list my experiences with X on my alpha.

This is to report that XFree86 4.2.1 on OpenBSD 3.2-RELEASE supports
the 3DFX Banshee on the Digital Personal Workstation 600 au. As it is,
I'm having some issues with the keyboard (I can move my mouse, but
typing in an xterm doesn't work, these probably end up on the console,
suggestions appreciated) but I'm sure I will be able to fix this
shortly.

This is all default installs - nothing strange. Insert official 3.2 CD
in drive, boot, install, create a new /etc/X11/XF86Config and startx -
right out of the box. See the attached dmesg, XF86Config and
XFree86.0.log for details.

I'm hoping to build some ports (GIMP/mplayer/tightvnc) soon to make
full use of my fine system ;)

Thanks to the developpers for making 3.2 another great release !

Paul 'WEiRD' de Weerd

